---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContentCTA from '../components/content/ContentCTA.astro';
import { marketingPages } from '../data/marketing-content';
import '../styles/content.css';
const meta = marketingPages.find((page) => page.path === '/about')!;
---
<Layout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} active="about" breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about' }]}>
  <div class="content-page container">
    <header class="content-about-hero"><span class="content-kicker">ABOUT FETCHX</span><h1>The web is complicated.<br/><span>Your access layer<br/>should be clear.</span></h1><p class="content-lead">FetchX is a web access API for applications that need to work with web sources.</p></header>
    <section class="content-about-purpose"><span class="content-kicker">THE TECHNICAL PURPOSE</span><div><h2>Make web access<br/>one dependable call.</h2><p>Web applications need more than a response body. They need the page, a clear reason when it could not be fetched, and a bill that matches what they got.</p><p>FetchX answers with one call: the cheapest route that works, a browser and captcha solving when needed, every attempt reported, and a charge only for success.</p><p>The caller keeps control of discovery, parsing, scheduling and application acceptance. That boundary lets the transport layer and the data pipeline be evaluated independently.</p></div></section>
    <section class="content-benefits content-about-values" aria-label="Engineering principles"><article><span class="content-number">01</span><h2>Cheapest first</h2><p>Plain HTTP before proxies, proxies before a browser. Each request climbs only as far as the site requires, within the credits you allow.</p></article><article><span class="content-number">02</span><h2>Clear outcomes</h2><p>A failure names what happened and the next step. A lost response is resent safely: the same key returns the first result, charged once.</p></article><article><span class="content-number">03</span><h2>Honest boundaries</h2><p>Fetching a page and having the right data are separate claims. FetchX reports what the site answered; your application decides what it means.</p></article></section>
    <section class="content-about-open"><div><span class="content-kicker">API DOCUMENTATION</span><h2>Read the docs.<br/>See how requests work.</h2></div><div><p>The FetchX documentation covers the API, auto mode, errors, billing and webhooks, with JavaScript and Python clients.</p><div class="content-actions"><a class="content-text-link" href="/docs">Read the API documentation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="content-text-link" href="/contact">Contact FetchX <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section>
    <ContentCTA/>
  </div>
</Layout>
